@nats-fleet-integration
Feature: NATS Fleet Integration
  As the Architect Agent
  I want to announce my capabilities, handle commands, publish heartbeats,
    and respond to agent-to-agent feasibility checks via nats-core
  So that I am a discoverable, callable fleet citizen alongside Jarvis
    and other specialist agents

  Background:
    Given a NATS server is running
    And the Architect Agent is configured with valid AgentConfig

  # ━━ GROUP A: Key Examples ━━

  # Why: Core lifecycle start — the agent must announce itself to the fleet
  # before it can receive any commands
  @key-example @smoke
  Scenario: Agent publishes its manifest on startup
    When the Architect Agent starts in serve mode
    Then an AgentManifest should be published to "fleet.register"
    And the manifest agent_id should be "architect-agent"
    And the manifest should declare 3 intent capabilities
    And the manifest should declare 4 tool capabilities
    And the manifest trust_tier should be "specialist"

  # Why: Core lifecycle end — clean deregistration prevents stale entries
  # in the ManifestRegistry
  @key-example @smoke
  Scenario: Agent deregisters on graceful shutdown
    Given the Architect Agent is registered with the fleet
    When the Architect Agent receives a shutdown signal
    Then an AgentDeregistrationPayload should be published to "fleet.deregister"
    And the deregistration agent_id should be "architect-agent"
    And the NATS connection should be closed

  # Why: Heartbeat is the liveness signal — without it, the fleet
  # considers the agent dead after the timeout window
  # [ASSUMPTION: confidence=high] Heartbeat interval is 30 seconds per AgentConfig default
  @key-example @smoke
  Scenario: Agent publishes heartbeats at the configured interval
    Given the Architect Agent is registered with the fleet
    When the heartbeat interval elapses
    Then an AgentHeartbeatPayload should be published to "fleet.heartbeat.architect-agent"
    And the heartbeat status should reflect the agent's current state
    And the heartbeat should include the current queue depth

  # Why: Core command handling — greenfield is the primary Mode 1 operation
  # and validates the full command-dispatch-result cycle
  @key-example @smoke
  Scenario: Agent receives and executes a greenfield command
    Given the Architect Agent is registered with the fleet
    When a CommandPayload arrives on "agents.command.architect-agent"
      | field          | value                 |
      | command        | greenfield            |
      | args.docs_path | /path/to/product-docs |
      | args.scope     | full                  |
      | correlation_id | cmd-abc-123           |
    Then the agent should invoke the greenfield session handler
    And a ResultPayload should be published to "agents.result.architect-agent"
    And the result correlation_id should be "cmd-abc-123"
    And the result success should be true

  # Why: Mode 2 alignment is request-reply, no Coach loop — validates
  # the synchronous command path distinct from async greenfield
  @key-example
  Scenario: Agent receives and executes an alignment command
    Given the Architect Agent is registered with the fleet
    When a CommandPayload arrives with command "align"
      | field          | value                                     |
      | args.context   | System uses event-driven architecture      |
      | args.proposal  | Add synchronous REST gateway              |
      | args.question  | Does this contradict our async-first ADR? |
      | correlation_id | cmd-align-456                             |
    Then the agent should invoke the alignment session handler
    And a ResultPayload should be published with correlation_id "cmd-align-456"

  # Why: Exploration is Mode 3 — read-only codebase analysis, validates
